  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain moves in late tonight from west to east. Locally\nheavy rainfall may occur over the Central Highlands after midnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure remains over the area today before building south\nand east this afternoon, allowing return flow to move into the\narea. PW values increase above 1.50 inches over srn and ern\nareas late tonight. These values are well above the 90th\npercentile for this time of year. At the same time LLJ of\n35-40kts increases between 09-15z from the Central Highlands \nnorth and east into ern Aroostook County. This will likely\nresult in locally heavy rainfall with very efficient warm rain\nprocesses. Cannot rule out ponding of water on roadways with\nhydroplaning and nuisance flooding expected.  \n\nExpect that storms will be few and far between but any that can\ndevelop will likely produce torrential downpours. Overall\nwidespread rainfall amounts look to range from 0.50-0.75 inches\nwith most of it falling late Sunday night in the pre-dawn areas\nto a few hours after sunrise. Cannot rule out localized higher\namounts. \n\nRegarding coastal low development deterministic guidance, \nincluding ensemble members, are fairly persistent that a low\nwill develop. However, it continues to slow down the \ndevelopment near the waters with most likely timeframe expected \nto be after 00z Tuesday. While this may cause enhanced rainfall\nto be delayed over Downeast it is also shifting lopres further \nto the south with heaviest rain possibly falling over the \nwaters. Confidence is decreasing with heaviest rainfall over \nDowneast at this time with higher amounts from the Central\nHighlands and back to the northwest. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another round of rain Wednesday afternoon into \nThursday as a cold front crosses the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA shortwave approaches from the west Wednesday behind the \ndeparting low pressure, bringing a cold front through the area. \nThe cold front will push a round of showers through the area \novernight into Thursday. Rainfall totals will likely be less \nthan 0.5 inches, with less for the Downeast. There could be some\nrumbles of thunder in the north, but no severe storms are \nexpected.",
